.TH "CSSMATHPRODUCT" "3JS" "August 11, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CSSMathProduct \- CSSMathProduct
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]CSSMathProduct\f[B]\f[R] interface of the CSS Typed
Object Model API represents the product of two or more
\f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] values \(em in cases where the result
can\(cqt be represented as a single value.
.SH CONSTRUCTOR
.TP
\f[B]CSSMathProduct()\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Creates a new \f[CR]CSSMathProduct\f[R] object.
.SH INSTANCE PROPERTIES
\f[I]Also inherits properties from its parent interface,
\f[CI]CSSMathValue\f[I].\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]CSSMathProduct.values\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R]
Returns a \f[CR]CSSNumericArray\f[R] object which contains one or more
\f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] objects.
.SH STATIC METHODS
\f[I]Also inherits methods from its parent interface,
\f[CI]CSSMathValue\f[I].\f[R]
.SH INSTANCE METHODS
\f[I]Also inherits methods from its parent interface,
\f[CI]CSSMathValue\f[I].\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A \f[CR]CSSMathProduct\f[R] is produced whenever a multiplication or
division can\(cqt be resolved to a single value \(em this happens when
more than one operand carries a unit, for example, multiplying two
lengths (\f[CR]10px * 20px\f[R]) or a length by a percentage, rather
than a value and a plain number.
.PP
Calling \f[CR]mul()\f[R] or \f[CR]div()\f[R] on operands that can\(cqt
be combined returns a \f[CR]CSSMathProduct\f[R]; if every operand is a
plain number, or all but one of them are, they resolve immediately to a
single \f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R] instead.
.PP
\f[CR]StylePropertyMapReadOnly.get()\f[R] returns a
\f[CR]CSSMathProduct\f[R] the same way \(em for a \f[CR]calc()\f[R]
value that resolves to a multiplication or division it can\(cqt combine
into one value.
.PP
\f[CR]CSSMathProduct\f[R] represents the product expression itself, not
a resolved value.
To get the resolved value, use \f[CR]getComputedStyle()\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
The following code creates a \f[CR]CSSMathProduct\f[R] instance from two
values, then reads back its \f[CR]operator\f[R] and \f[CR]values\f[R]
properties.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] product = \f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathProduct(CSS.px(10), CSS.percent(50));

console.log(product.constructor.name); \f[I]// \(dqCSSMathProduct\(dq\f[R]
console.log(product.operator); \f[I]// \(aqproduct\(aq\f[R]
console.log(product.values); \f[I]// CSSNumericArray {0: CSSUnitValue, 1: CSSUnitValue, length: 2}\f[R]
console.log(product.values[0]); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 10, unit: \(dqpx\(dq}\f[R]
.EE
.SS \f[CR]calc()\f[R] representations
This example shows how a \f[CR]calc()\f[R] multiplication is represented
by a \f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R] or a \f[CR]CSSMathProduct\f[R], depending
on whether it can be resolved to a single value.
.SS HTML
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqdemoBox\(dq>Text</\f[B]div\f[R]>
.EE
.SS CSS
\f[CR]width\f[R] is set using a \f[CR]calc()\f[R] product of a length
and a plain number, so the browser can resolve it to a single fixed
value immediately.
\f[CR]font\-size\f[R] is set using a \f[CR]calc()\f[R] product that
multiplies a plain number by a parenthesized sum of \f[CR]1rem\f[R] and
\f[CR]5vw\f[R]; since the sum itself can\(cqt be combined into a single
value (it mixes units), the product can\(cqt either, and this will be
represented by a \f[CR]CSSMathProduct\f[R].
.IP
.EX
demoBox {
  \f[B]width\f[R]: calc(10px * 2);
  \f[B]font\-size\f[R]: calc(2 * (1rem + 5vw));
}
.EE
.SS JavaScript
First we find the demo box\(cqs style rule and read its \f[CR]width\f[R]
and \f[CR]font\-size\f[R] values using \f[CR]styleMap\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] demoBox = document.querySelector(\(dq#demoBox\(dq);

\f[B]const\f[R] rules = document.getElementById(\(dqcss\-output\(dq).sheet.cssRules;
\f[B]const\f[R] rule = [...rules].find((r) \f[B]=>\f[R] r.selectorText === \(dq#demoBox\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] styleMap = rule.styleMap;
\f[B]const\f[R] width = styleMap.get(\(dqwidth\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] fontSize = styleMap.get(\(dqfont\-size\(dq);
.EE
.PP
We then log the type and value of the CSS Typed OM representations,
followed by the computed (resolved) values.
.IP
.EX
log(\(dqwidth\(dq);
log(\(ga type: ${width.constructor.name}\(ga);
log(\(ga value: ${width}\(ga);
log(\(ga resolved: ${getComputedStyle(demoBox).width}\(ga);

log(\(dq\(rsnfont\-size\(dq);
log(\(ga type: ${fontSize.constructor.name}\(ga);
log(\(ga values: [${[...fontSize.values].join(\(dq, \(dq)}]\(ga);
log(\(ga resolved: ${getComputedStyle(demoBox).fontSize}\(ga);
.EE
.SS Result
\f[CR]width\f[R] is represented by a \f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R] object,
which has a value that matches the resolved width.
\f[CR]font\-size\f[R] is represented by a \f[CR]CSSMathProduct\f[R]
object that exposes the \f[CR]calc()\f[R] product\(cqs original terms.
